rbatim\nL876@P11: to formal series. In the same fashion, the analogue of Qp is the field of Laurent series Fp((t)).\nL877@P11: Noticing further that Fp[t] is dense in Fp[[t]] and similarly that Fp(t) is dense in Fp((t)) 3, we can\nL878@P11: even supplement the correspondence between the characteristic 0 and the characteristic p, ending\nL879@P11: up with the “dictionary” of Figure 1.5.\nL880@P11: Algebraists have actually managed to capture the essence of there resemblances in the notion of\nL881@P11: discrete valuation rings/fields and completion of those [74]. Recently, Scholze defined the notion of\nL882@P11: perfectoid which allows us (under several additional assumptions) to build an actual bridge between\nL883@P11: the characteristic 0 and the characteristic p. It is not the purpose of this lecture to go further in\nL884@P11: this direction; we nevertheless refer interested people to [71, 72, 9] for the exposition of the theory.\nL885@P11: 3The natural embedding Fp(t) → Fp((t)) takes a rational function to its Taylor expansion at 0.\nL886@P11-12: II–11X. Caruso\nL887@P12: 1.3.1. The point of view of algebraic geometry. Algebraic Geometry has an original and interesting\nL888@P12: point of view on the various rings and fields introduced before, showing in particular how p-adic\nL889@P12: numbers can arise in many natural problems in arithmetics. The underlying ubiquitous idea in\nL890@P12: algebraic geometry is to associate to any ring A a geometrical space SpecA — its so-called spectrum\nL891@P12: — on which the functions are the elements of A. We will not detail here the general construction of\nL892@P12: Spec A but just try to explain informally what it looks like when A is one of the rings appearing in\nL893@P12: Figure 1.5.\nL894@P12: Let us start with k[t] where k is a field (of any characteristic). For the simplicity of the exposition,\nL895@P12: let us assume further that k is algebraically closed. One thinks of elements of k[t] as (polynomial)\nL896@P12: functions over k, meaning that this spectrum should be thought of as k itself. Spec k[t] is called the\nL897@P12: affine line over k and is usually drawn as a straight line. The spectrum of k(t) can be understood\nL898@P12: in similar terms: a rational fraction f ∈ k(t) defines a function on k as well, except that it can be\nL899@P12: undefined at some points. Therefore Spec k(t) might be thought as the affine line over k with a\nL900@P12: “moving” finite set of points removed. It is called the generic point of the affine line.\nL901@P12: What about k[[t]]? If k has characteristic 0 (which we assume for the simplicity of the discussion),\nL902@P12: the datum of f ∈ k[[t]] is equivalent to the datum of the values of the f\nL903@P12: (n)\nL904@P12: (0)’s (for n varying\nL905@P12: in N); we sometimes say that f defines a function on a formal neighborhood of 0. This formal\nL906@P12: neighborhood is the spectrum of k[[t]]; it should then be thought as a kind of thickening of the\nL907@P12: point 0 ∈ Spec k[t] which does not include any other point (since a formal series f ∈ k[[t]] cannot\nL908@P12: be evaluated in general at any other point than 0). Finally Spec k((t)) is the punctured formal\nL909@P12: neighborhood of 0; it is obtained from Spec k[[t]] by removing the point 0 but not its neighborhood!\nL910@P12: The embedding k[t] → k[[t]] (resp. k(t) → k((t))) given by Taylor expansion at 0 corresponds to\nL911@P12: the restriction of a function f defined on the affine line (resp. the generic point of the affine line)\nL912@P12: to the formal neighborhood (resp. the punctured formal neighborhood) of 0.\nL913@P12: Of course, one can define similarly formal neighborhoods and punctured formal neighborhoods\n"
